WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.468 --> 00:00:03.610
Hello! Welcome to "Mathematical Adventures".

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:06.825
Today, we're diving deep into the prime number theorem,

00:00:06.825 --> 00:00:08.381
which goes something like this:

00:00:08.381 --> 00:00:11.335
The number of primes less than n

00:00:11.335 --> 00:00:15.454
is roughly n divided by Log[n].

00:00:15.454 --> 00:00:18.418
In fancy math talk, it sounds like this:

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:22.798
The limit of this ratio equals 1.

00:00:22.798 --> 00:00:26.237
Now, we won't be proving this theorem today; it's quite the mathematical odyssey.

00:00:26.237 --> 00:00:28.647
Instead, we'll prove a pair of these nifty inequalities.

00:00:28.647 --> 00:00:32.918
We want to prove that this expression is sandwiched between two positive numbers, a and b.

00:00:32.918 --> 00:00:36.280
Concrete, real-deal positive numbers.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.093
This problem has occupied the minds of some mathematical legends, including Pafnuty Chebyshev.

00:00:39.093 --> 00:00:43.110
Yes, the same fellow who brought us those polynomials we talked about recently.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.858
He came up with this result back in 1852.

00:00:46.858 --> 00:00:53.542
The constants a and b he found were pretty darn close to 1.

00:00:53.542 --> 00:00:58.840
But he couldn't prove the existence of the limit, even though he was on the right track.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.883
He did prove, however, that if there is a limit, it's equal to 1.

00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:07.450
A rather intriguing claim.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:12.425
The actual proof of this limit's existence came 45 years later.

00:01:12.425 --> 00:01:17.400
And it was based on the Riemann zeta function.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.888
Specifically, the fact that it has a pole at 1,

00:01:20.888 --> 00:01:28.980
and all of its zeros reside to the left of a vertical line with a real part of 1.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.843
But, my friends, that's getting into some serious mathematical territory...

00:01:31.843 --> 00:01:34.957
Far easier to derive these nifty inequalities instead.

00:01:35.963 --> 00:01:40.000
This one on the right is a walk in the park to get.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.929
We'll be using school methods and those Catalan numbers we learnt about recently.

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:52.965
In general, I'd like this video to be more than just a proof.

00:01:52.965 --> 00:01:57.336
I want it to be a mathematical journey.

00:01:57.336 --> 00:02:00.000
You see, we stumbled upon Catalan numbers.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.150
We noticed some fascinating prime-related properties hiding within these Catalans.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.502
So, we decided to explore further.

00:02:07.502 --> 00:02:12.979
By pure chance, we stumbled upon one inequality and then another.

00:02:12.979 --> 00:02:20.000
The goal of this video is to showcase this mathematical adventure as a way of the Samurai.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:27.124
When there's no fixed destination, just new mathematical terrain to explore and intriguing observations to make.

00:02:27.124 --> 00:02:31.797
We follow the mathematical path illuminated by curiosity.

00:02:31.797 --> 00:02:35.619
We gather important observations along the way.

00:02:35.619 --> 00:02:40.744
And these accumulated insights will surely come in handy when tackling specific mathematical puzzles on our journey.

00:02:40.744 --> 00:02:47.076
These observations will be our guiding light as we continue on this mathematical adventure.

00:02:47.076 --> 00:02:50.593
In addition to these inequalities, we'll uncover more mathematical gems.

00:02:50.593 --> 00:02:53.858
And some visually stunning plots...

00:02:53.858 --> 00:02:56.319
Like this one, for instance. What does it mean?

00:02:56.319 --> 00:02:59.014
Let me try to explain right away.

00:02:59.014 --> 00:03:05.347
Take the Catalan numbers and look at the powers of 2 within them.

00:03:05.347 --> 00:03:08.411
In the Catalan numbers c1, c2, c3, and so on.

00:03:08.411 --> 00:03:13.134
Apply Accumulate to this series of numbers.

00:03:13.134 --> 00:03:18.058
Then subtract (n+1)Log[n + 1] from it.

00:03:18.058 --> 00:03:23.786
And voilà, you've got yourself this beautiful fractal function.

00:03:23.786 --> 00:03:27.906
And if we divide it by n, we get this intriguing result.

00:03:27.906 --> 00:03:37.304
In essence, it depicts the function of the power of the prime number 2 in the product of the first n Catalan numbers.

00:03:37.304 --> 00:03:43.215
Also minus a certain base function of (n+1)Log[n+1].

00:03:43.215 --> 00:03:45.626
Alright, let's keep this mathematical journey rolling.

00:03:45.626 --> 00:03:52.466
So, we've uncovered the Catalan numbers (c_n) and their explicit formula.

00:03:52.466 --> 00:03:59.401
But today, we'll find it more convenient to work with numbers that are n + 1 times larger.

00:03:59.401 --> 00:04:02.701
In other words, with the binomial coefficient 2n/n.

00:04:02.701 --> 00:04:04.601
Let's define it.

00:04:04.601 --> 00:04:07.112
Here are our Catalan numbers.

00:04:07.112 --> 00:04:10.000
And here are the first 10 centered binomial coefficients.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:20.000
I'll call them centered binomial coefficients because these numbers, 2, 6, 20, 70...

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.898
They are these numbers: 2, 6, 20, 70...

00:04:26.898 --> 00:04:30.895
Let's enclose them in a frame.

00:04:30.895 --> 00:04:36.624
In general, today we'll be studying these binomial coefficients,

00:04:36.624 --> 00:04:39.577
investigating the powers of prime numbers within them,

00:04:39.577 --> 00:04:43.043
and making interesting observations.

00:04:43.043 --> 00:04:44.749
Here's observation №1.

00:04:44.749 --> 00:04:52.690
The remainders when dividing these binomial coefficients by 5 look like this.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:56.985
Some remainders go on, and then there's a long sequence of zeros.

00:04:56.985 --> 00:05:00.000
Again, non-zeros,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.381
then another long sequence of zeros.

00:05:03.381 --> 00:05:08.550
This holds true for both 7 and 11...

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:13.073
We keep encountering these long sequences of zeros all the time.

00:05:13.073 --> 00:05:16.238
Let's delve into these zeros.

00:05:16.238 --> 00:05:19.072
A zero signifies divisibility by a prime number, p.

00:05:19.072 --> 00:05:25.312
But to what power, precisely, does the number b divide by p?

00:05:25.312 --> 00:05:36.822
To understand this, we'll define the function vp, which represents the exponent of the prime number p in the integer x.

00:05:36.822 --> 00:05:40.330
For instance, let's consider the case of 5.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:46.210
Here, we have remainders "2, 1".

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.772
This implies that the number isn't divisible by 5.

00:05:48.772 --> 00:05:50.427
Then "0, 0".

00:05:50.427 --> 00:05:55.594
This indicates that the number is divisible by 5, and specifically, it's divisible by the first power of 5.

00:05:55.594 --> 00:05:57.833
Then it's not divisible again, and then it is...

00:05:57.833 --> 00:06:00.696
We encounter numbers that are divisible by 5^2,

00:06:00.696 --> 00:06:02.403
and 5^3.

00:06:02.403 --> 00:06:07.478
In fact, if desired, you can find numbers that are divisible by any power of 5.

00:06:07.478 --> 00:06:13.524
However, the maximum power of 5 in binomial coefficients increases very slowly.

00:06:13.524 --> 00:06:16.437
This is an interesting observation.

00:06:16.437 --> 00:06:20.000
What's also interesting is that we periodically encounter zeros here.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.673
So, no matter how far we go in these binomial coefficients,

00:06:24.673 --> 00:06:30.553
numbers that are not divisible by 5 will still appear.

00:06:30.553 --> 00:06:31.940
So...

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:37.970
We expand using the formula b_n and get something such,

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:45.509
that the exponent of the prime number p in b_n is equal to the difference between these two expressions.

00:06:45.509 --> 00:06:50.465
The function vp is an entirely additive arithmetic function.

00:06:50.465 --> 00:06:55.339
vp[a * b] = vp[a] + vp[b]

00:06:55.339 --> 00:06:59.107
And when we divide, we get the difference.

00:06:59.107 --> 00:07:03.730
In essence, today we'll try to express this equation.

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:09.560
I've called it the vp-identity, but that's an unofficial name. It's incorrect.

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:16.999
What we want to write down is that x can be expressed as a product of prime powers in the following way.

00:07:16.999 --> 00:07:19.208
We simply take the Log[x].

00:07:19.208 --> 00:07:26.279
Then, instead of a product, we have the sum of Log[p] times the power of the prime number p within x.

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:34.873
And we'll apply this elementary equation to various expressions to get what we need.

00:07:34.873 --> 00:07:38.395
But let's start with something very simple for now.

00:07:38.395 --> 00:07:44.777
Let's immediately derive a fascinating result by school methods.

00:07:44.777 --> 00:07:47.063
The result is as follows:

00:07:47.063 --> 00:07:56.009
It's clear that the central binomial coefficient is less than 2^2n.

00:07:56.009 --> 00:08:00.899
So, let's consider this expansion.

00:08:00.899 --> 00:08:03.451
The 10th row of Pascal's triangle.

00:08:03.451 --> 00:08:06.867
Let's take this central binomial coefficient...

00:08:06.867 --> 00:08:11.540
And it's entirely obvious that it's less than 2^10.

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:18.224
Simply because the sum of all coefficients (this is x = 1) yields 2^10.

00:08:18.224 --> 00:08:22.193
So, on one hand, we have obvious inequalities,

00:08:22.193 --> 00:08:33.150
and on the other hand, from the formula, we can see that in b_n, prime numbers greater than n are in the numerator.

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:39.373
And they cannot be canceled by what's in the denominator.

00:08:39.373 --> 00:08:43.543
(2n)! is the product of numbers from 1 to 2n.

00:08:43.543 --> 00:08:49.121
n! is the product of all numbers from 1 to n, and there are two of them.

00:08:49.121 --> 00:08:54.092
Prime numbers greater than n cannot appear in the denominator.

00:08:54.092 --> 00:09:06.960
This means that b_n is definitely greater than the product of all prime numbers from n + 1 (inclusive) to 2n.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:10.326
So, this is what we want to write down.

00:09:10.326 --> 00:09:18.518
2^2n is greater than the product of prime numbers from n + 1 to 2n.

00:09:18.518 --> 00:09:21.230
A result suitable for school.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:23.809
Let's introduce a definition...

00:09:23.809 --> 00:09:26.792
Alright... let's call this function θ[n].

00:09:26.792 --> 00:09:36.534
And let it be the product of all prime numbers less than n, along with the logarithm.

00:09:36.534 --> 00:09:40.334
The logarithm of a product is expressed as the sum of logarithms.

00:09:40.334 --> 00:09:45.811
So, when we take the logarithm of both sides,

00:09:45.811 --> 00:09:48.322
it gives us...

00:09:48.322 --> 00:09:51.034
Well... this 4^n here.

00:09:51.034 --> 00:10:02.294
This means Log[4n] ≥ (θ[2n] - θ[n]).

00:10:02.294 --> 00:10:09.431
Let's write this down several times for n, n/2, n/4, n/8, and so on.

00:10:09.431 --> 00:10:14.255
Let's write down Log₂[n] lines here.

00:10:14.255 --> 00:10:16.801
And let's sum up these inequalities.

00:10:16.801 --> 00:10:18.387
What will we get?

00:10:18.387 --> 00:10:23.060
On the left, we get Log[4] * 2n.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:29.241
2n is the sum of all these factors plus one.

00:10:29.241 --> 00:10:31.819
Here, we get cancellation.

00:10:31.819 --> 00:10:36.592
This cancels out with this, and so on.

00:10:36.592 --> 00:10:39.455
And we are left with just the first part.

00:10:39.455 --> 00:10:44.882
In the end, Log[4]2n ≥ θ[2n]

00:10:44.882 --> 00:10:53.426
Now, when we substitute n/2 here, we get θ[n] ≤ Log[4]n.

00:10:53.426 --> 00:10:58.517
Approximately ≈ 1.386 * n

00:10:58.517 --> 00:11:02.706
By the way, we've already dealt with this function in one of our videos.

00:11:02.706 --> 00:11:05.373
And got this experimental result.

00:11:05.373 --> 00:11:08.967
That θ[n] is actually ≈ n.

00:11:08.967 --> 00:11:14.293
And it becomes slightly less than n asymptotically.

00:11:14.293 --> 00:11:21.027
So, the truth is that θ[n] has an asymptote.

00:11:21.027 --> 00:11:24.745
If we divide θ[n] by n, in the limit, we get 1.

00:11:24.745 --> 00:11:29.672
This theorem is, in fact, equivalent to the Prime Number Theorem.

00:11:29.672 --> 00:11:37.160
We saw it through numerical experimentation, but we didn't prove it.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:45.518
In short, the truth is that instead of 1.386, we should write 1, but it's not that simple.

00:11:45.518 --> 00:11:50.643
So far, we've got this upper bound.

00:11:50.643 --> 00:11:52.274
Let's move on.

00:11:52.274 --> 00:11:59.078
This result can be further developed.

00:11:59.078 --> 00:12:02.444
Somehow, the idea comes to us...

00:12:02.444 --> 00:12:08.877
Let's take the product of prime numbers from √n to n.

00:12:08.877 --> 00:12:12.701
How does this idea come to our minds?

00:12:12.701 --> 00:12:15.054
Well... just think about it.

00:12:15.054 --> 00:12:21.637
This idea actually comes to mind when you think about this result.

00:12:21.637 --> 00:12:31.452
You start to understand that if we take θ[√n], it's asymptotically smaller than θ[n].

00:12:31.452 --> 00:12:41.422
So, we can study prime numbers from √n to n.

00:12:41.422 --> 00:12:43.080
What will we get?

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:48.306
We'll get that this logarithm is equal to the difference.

00:12:48.306 --> 00:12:55.932
We can get a lower estimate like this:

00:12:55.932 --> 00:12:58.515
how many of these numbers from √n to n?

00:12:58.515 --> 00:13:01.277
This difference is already the Pi function.

00:13:01.277 --> 00:13:06.754
That's the number of prime numbers in this range.

00:13:08.754 --> 00:13:12.011
So, we take their products and logarithms.

00:13:14.424 --> 00:13:18.221
It's like we're taking the sum of the logarithms of these prime numbers.

00:13:18.221 --> 00:13:23.397
And each of these terms is definitely greater than Log[√n].

00:13:23.397 --> 00:13:26.541
So, we write down this expression and say that it's less.

00:13:26.541 --> 00:13:30.526
We've got a lower estimate for this thing.

00:13:30.526 --> 00:13:38.417
Next, we divide by Log[√n] and get this.

00:13:38.417 --> 00:13:46.033
Then we move Pi[√n] to the right and make a very rough estimate.

00:13:46.033 --> 00:13:51.714
The number of prime numbers less than √n is definitely less than √n.

00:13:51.714 --> 00:14:00.466
And in the end, we can discard this term by rounding up the "α" coefficient.

00:14:00.466 --> 00:14:08.298
And say that, starting from some large n, we can neglect this term

00:14:08.298 --> 00:14:12.410
due to a slight increase in this constant.

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:17.578
In the end, we've got an upper estimate for Pi[n].

00:14:17.578 --> 00:14:29.143
But the truth is, instead of 1.1 (like Chebyshev), we got 2.78, much cruder. But it was a complete giveaway.

00:14:29.143 --> 00:14:34.818
That's how we quickly got two results.

00:14:34.818 --> 00:14:37.687
One for the θ[n] function,

00:14:37.687 --> 00:14:40.450
and the other for the Pi[n] function.

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:43.162
Okay...

00:14:43.162 --> 00:14:45.572
Let's move on.

00:14:45.572 --> 00:15:01.708
Let's write down this vp - identity for n! and the binomial coefficient b_n.

00:15:01.708 --> 00:15:08.445
Let's start with the function vp[n], where n ranges from 1 to 128.

00:15:08.445 --> 00:15:11.726
Take the prime number 2.

00:15:11.726 --> 00:15:18.209
This is what the powers of the prime number 2 in the number n look like.

00:15:18.209 --> 00:15:19.664
A simple function like this.

00:15:19.664 --> 00:15:24.840
Every second number isn't divisible by 2, so vp = 0.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:29.799
Every second number is divisible by 2. Here...

00:15:29.799 --> 00:15:34.806
Every fourth number is divisible by 2^2.

00:15:34.806 --> 00:15:39.672
Every eighth number is divisible by 2^3, and so on.

00:15:39.672 --> 00:15:48.689
And vp[n!] is actually the sum of all these columns.

00:15:50.650 --> 00:15:52.117
Moving on...

00:15:52.117 --> 00:15:56.488
We can calculate the sum of these columns like this:

00:15:56.488 --> 00:16:02.317
We can calculate it horizontally.

00:16:02.317 --> 00:16:05.230
Let me explain...

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:12.215
Let's mentally imagine that there are points at the intersections with the horizontals as well.

00:16:12.215 --> 00:16:21.161
Then, the sum of the heights of these columns equals the number of points in this region.

00:16:21.161 --> 00:16:25.971
In other words, above horizontal 0.

00:16:25.971 --> 00:16:31.014
So, vp[n!] can be calculated like this:

00:16:31.014 --> 00:16:34.117
the number of points on horizontal 1,

00:16:34.117 --> 00:16:36.117
the number of points on horizontal 2,

00:16:36.117 --> 00:16:39.445
including these intersections.

00:16:39.445 --> 00:16:44.547
The number of points on horizontal 3, and so on.

00:16:44.547 --> 00:16:58.073
If n = p^k, then the number of points on the first horizontal equals (p^k)/p, which is p^k-1.

00:16:58.073 --> 00:17:08.654
The number of points on the second horizontal equals n/(p^2), which is p^k - 2 if n = p^k.

00:17:08.654 --> 00:17:10.945
And so on...

00:17:10.945 --> 00:17:15.525
So, for a specific value of n = p^k

00:17:15.525 --> 00:17:24.348
vp[n!] becomes a geometric progression with q = p.

00:17:24.348 --> 00:17:25.090
Moving on...

00:17:25.090 --> 00:17:29.422
Now let's think about b_n.

00:17:29.422 --> 00:17:35.352
There we have vp[(2n)!] equal to this.

00:17:35.352 --> 00:17:38.080
vp[n!] equals this.

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:44.601
And we need to subtract two of these. We get 0.

00:17:44.601 --> 00:17:53.799
At these points, the remainder of division by p isn't zero for n = p^k.

00:17:53.799 --> 00:17:57.466
For other n that are not equal to p^k,

00:17:57.466 --> 00:18:04.703
the number of points on the k-th horizontal equals n/p^k, rounded down.

00:18:04.703 --> 00:18:12.845
These square brackets represent rounding down of a real number.

00:18:12.845 --> 00:18:19.606
You can consider this variant where n = (p^k) plus some small a.

00:18:19.606 --> 00:18:27.751
And then we get this expression for vp[n!]

00:18:27.751 --> 00:18:32.173
and this expression for vp[(2n)!].

00:18:32.173 --> 00:18:37.399
When we subtract one from the other,

00:18:37.399 --> 00:18:41.619
these first parts cancel out

00:18:41.619 --> 00:18:44.524
and only the second parts remain,

00:18:44.524 --> 00:18:48.543
and we can group them further like this.

00:18:48.543 --> 00:18:51.758
We end up with a sum of such brackets.

00:18:51.758 --> 00:18:57.571
Well... instead of a, you could write n here. It's not that important.

00:18:57.571 --> 00:19:05.914
So, this function can be represented as the sum of such functions,

00:19:05.914 --> 00:19:08.676
which are essentially the same function f[x],

00:19:08.676 --> 00:19:18.879
but for x it's first a/p, then a/(p^2), then a/(p^3).

00:19:21.697 --> 00:19:27.351
By the way, these square brackets work directly in Mathematica.

00:19:27.351 --> 00:19:30.414
Here's the function I'm talking about.

00:19:30.414 --> 00:19:36.599
Floor[2x] - 2Floor[x]

00:19:36.599 --> 00:19:40.000
Let's define it.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:48.249
And, for example, for the prime number 3, we'll have this option for the first bracket.

00:19:48.249 --> 00:19:56.439
Such a thing for the sum of the first two brackets. And so on...

00:19:56.439 --> 00:20:02.414
Look... we get a kind of fractal function.

00:20:02.414 --> 00:20:05.070
Let's see how it's structured.

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:08.726
First, we have these three platforms.

00:20:08.726 --> 00:20:13.248
Then, we divide each platform into triangles.

00:20:13.248 --> 00:20:15.106
Here the triangle goes up,

00:20:15.106 --> 00:20:16.360
while here the triangle goes down,

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:19.575
and then the triangle goes up again.

00:20:19.575 --> 00:20:25.345
We essentially break down each platform into three subplatforms.

00:20:25.345 --> 00:20:29.263
Here, the middle one goes up,

00:20:29.263 --> 00:20:32.026
while here, the two edges go up.

00:20:32.026 --> 00:20:35.140
Here, the middle one goes up again.

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:37.148
And the same process continues.

00:20:37.148 --> 00:20:40.464
Next, we again divide each of these subplatforms into three parts,

00:20:40.464 --> 00:20:49.150
and again, every other one is lifted up.

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:52.918
For five, the same concept applies.

00:20:52.918 --> 00:21:02.267
Only there, each platform is divided into five pieces, and two subplatforms go up

00:21:02.267 --> 00:21:07.141
or three subplatforms go up.

00:21:07.141 --> 00:21:09.419
Here, it's two again.

00:21:11.782 --> 00:21:12.936
And so on.

00:21:12.936 --> 00:21:22.870
In short, it's quite understandable how the function vp for the number b_n is structured.

00:21:22.870 --> 00:21:28.379
Here, each point x holds b_x,

00:21:28.379 --> 00:21:31.536
and the Y-axis represents the value of vp,

00:21:31.536 --> 00:21:36.603
the power of the prime number p within b_x.

00:21:36.603 --> 00:21:44.793
We get exactly what we analytically predicted.

00:21:44.793 --> 00:21:46.147
The next observation.

00:21:46.147 --> 00:21:50.317
When we take a large number of b_n values,

00:21:50.317 --> 00:21:59.565
we see that the function vp for them can be bounded from above by the logarithm of n base p.

00:21:59.565 --> 00:22:07.835
Plus some constant, which is unique for each prime but small (&lt;1).

00:22:07.835 --> 00:22:11.574
When we take the logarithmic X-axis,

00:22:11.574 --> 00:22:14.286
plot the logarithm of n here,

00:22:14.286 --> 00:22:18.959
and here, just vp of the number b_n,

00:22:18.959 --> 00:22:22.971
we get such a linear boundary.

00:22:22.971 --> 00:22:40.603
I added 0.1 here to show these blue dots, which are located directly below the yellow line if you don't add 0.1.

00:22:40.603 --> 00:22:41.316
So...

00:22:41.320 --> 00:22:48.356
For two, you get a very precise approximation of the maximum value of the vp function.

00:22:48.356 --> 00:22:51.873
But you need use Log[n+1] instead of Log[n].

00:22:51.873 --> 00:22:58.306
That extra 1 is added, I'll explain where it comes from later.

00:22:58.306 --> 00:23:01.607
For five, and for nineteen.

00:23:01.607 --> 00:23:12.313
Everywhere, you get a simple linear function if you take the logarithmic X-axis.

00:23:12.313 --> 00:23:14.120
Alright...

00:23:16.181 --> 00:23:20.218
vp grows slowly, logarithmically.

00:23:20.218 --> 00:23:24.600
And it takes on values 0, 1, 2, 3, ..., whole numbers.

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:29.030
The question arises: "How many blue dots are there on this line,

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:32.144
on this one, on this one, and so on?"

00:23:32.144 --> 00:23:38.024
That's exactly what we'll deal with today. And we'll get the following answers.

00:23:38.024 --> 00:23:47.070
The average value of Y for n = p^k is equal to this.

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:53.491
Approximately k/2 minus a small adjustment for p &gt; 2.

00:23:53.491 --> 00:23:56.684
And for two, it's a different result.

00:23:56.684 --> 00:23:59.698
We'll prove it directly.

00:23:59.698 --> 00:24:01.555
Let's go...

00:24:03.918 --> 00:24:10.814
You can count the number of different Y values using the Tally function.

00:24:10.814 --> 00:24:16.283
Look... Tally counts frequencies.

00:24:16.283 --> 00:24:18.392
1 occurs 5 times.

00:24:18.392 --> 00:24:20.000
2 occurs 3 times.

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:22.913
101 occurs 2 times.

00:24:22.913 --> 00:24:25.373
And here it's the same.

00:24:25.373 --> 00:24:29.091
But I prefer to use the IntTally function.

00:24:29.091 --> 00:24:30.898
How it works.

00:24:30.898 --> 00:24:37.079
It gives you the same frequencies but not as pairs, just as an array of frequencies.

00:24:37.079 --> 00:24:43.981
The i-th position contains how many times the number i occurs.

00:24:47.752 --> 00:24:50.480
Look at what we have...

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:56.859
How many times is vp[b[n]] for p = 2 equal to zero?

00:24:56.859 --> 00:24:58.616
Answer: 0 times.

00:24:58.616 --> 00:25:02.736
How many times is vp[b[n]] equal to 1?

00:25:02.736 --> 00:25:03.800
7 times.

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:05.335
How many times is it equal to 2?

00:25:05.335 --> 00:25:08.206
And so on...

00:25:08.206 --> 00:25:13.281
We consider all b_i, where i ranges from 1

00:25:13.281 --> 00:25:16.433
to such an n.

00:25:18.142 --> 00:25:23.709
Clearly, these are simply binomial coefficients.

00:25:23.709 --> 00:25:27.089
Interesting, right?

00:25:27.089 --> 00:25:31.963
So, in different binomial coefficients from b_1 to b_n,

00:25:31.963 --> 00:25:37.239
the frequencies of different values form a row from Pascal's Triangle.

00:25:37.239 --> 00:25:42.959
In other words, they are also binomial coefficients.

00:25:42.959 --> 00:25:55.882
Let's draw the frequencies of these powers of b_1 to b_n for different values of m, where n = (p^m) - 1.

00:25:55.882 --> 00:25:58.236
Here's Pascal's Triangle.

00:25:58.236 --> 00:26:04.917
Only with the ones detached in the first column.

00:26:04.917 --> 00:26:10.000
This triangle has a generating function.

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:11.930
Like this.

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:17.006
More precisely, it's the generating function of Pascal's Triangle itself without the detached ones.

00:26:17.006 --> 00:26:18.759
To detach the ones,

00:26:18.759 --> 00:26:23.834
you need to subtract 1/(1-y) here, 1 from here, 1 from here, and so on...

00:26:23.834 --> 00:26:31.442
So, in reality, you need to subtract 1/(1-y).

00:26:31.442 --> 00:26:38.292
This comes from the formula for an infinite geometric series.

00:26:38.292 --> 00:26:45.177
If you're not familiar with this, you'll have to look it up on your own.

00:26:45.177 --> 00:26:49.703
In general, the generating function for Pascal's Triangle is simple.

00:26:49.703 --> 00:26:54.800
For y^k, we want to see the k-th row.

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:57.159
In other words, (1 + x)^k.

00:26:57.159 --> 00:27:03.441
We want to see the generating function for the kth row in terms of the variable x.

00:27:03.441 --> 00:27:10.000
The generating function for the triangle is a function of two variables: x and y.

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:12.437
And it's structured like this.

00:27:12.437 --> 00:27:22.015
For y^k, we want to see a polynomial in x, whose coefficients are the numbers in the k-th row of Pascal's Triangle.

00:27:22.015 --> 00:27:27.008
Some triangle. Whichever you prefer.

00:27:27.008 --> 00:27:36.210
Here's the generating function for a triangle with the first column detached.

00:27:36.210 --> 00:27:38.889
Let's make sure of that.

00:27:38.889 --> 00:27:44.824
I divided by y again here because our triangle starts from the second row.

00:27:44.824 --> 00:27:46.944
Okay... Checking...

00:27:46.944 --> 00:27:48.735
Yes, indeed...

00:27:48.735 --> 00:27:50.251
There's no 1 here,

00:27:50.251 --> 00:27:51.186
no 1 here...

00:27:51.186 --> 00:27:53.136
The 1 is detached.

00:27:53.136 --> 00:27:55.496
Okay.

00:27:55.496 --> 00:28:00.873
We need to get a similar generating function for other prime numbers.

00:28:00.873 --> 00:28:02.865
3, 5 and so on...

00:28:02.865 --> 00:28:08.143
Then, we might come across something interesting.

00:28:08.143 --> 00:28:10.595
We'll get this result soon.

00:28:10.595 --> 00:28:14.463
It will be like this.

00:28:14.463 --> 00:28:22.059
And for p = 3, we'll have a generating function like this.

00:28:22.059 --> 00:28:25.890
And here are the rows of this triangle.

00:28:25.890 --> 00:28:31.704
Each row represents the frequencies of different values of vp

00:28:31.704 --> 00:28:36.338
when we consider vp[b1], vp[b2], ..., vp[bn],

00:28:36.338 --> 00:28:43.826
where n is equal to (p^k) - 1 (or not -1, we'll see).

00:28:43.826 --> 00:28:44.709
Alright...

00:28:44.709 --> 00:28:49.433
I must postpone the proof of this statement until the end of the video.

00:28:49.433 --> 00:28:55.359
For now, let's look at the experimental results.

00:28:55.359 --> 00:29:01.579
Let's experimentally study our vp function some more.

00:29:01.579 --> 00:29:11.736
And very often, when you have a sequence of numbers, it makes sense to consider partial sums of that sequence.

00:29:11.736 --> 00:29:18.520
To compute partial sums in Wolfram Mathematica, you use the Accumulate function.

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:30.000
Accumulate turns a sequence of numbers into a sequence of partial sums, where the k-th element is the sum of the first k.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:35.876
Here's what Accumulate looks like for p = 2.

00:29:35.876 --> 00:29:45.124
Accumulate of vp[b_i] is vp of the product b1, b2, b3, ..., b_n.

00:29:45.124 --> 00:29:48.189
We get this plot...

00:29:48.189 --> 00:29:53.717
And it's evident that it has some small "peaks",

00:29:53.717 --> 00:29:57.132
which are more noticeable for 3.

00:29:57.132 --> 00:30:01.654
There are such points. Maxima.

00:30:01.654 --> 00:30:09.317
For them, you can get the exact value of Accumulate[vp[b_i]].

00:30:09.317 --> 00:30:18.424
For two, in particular, it's simply 0.5Log[n+1] * (n+1).

00:30:18.424 --> 00:30:23.146
And when we subtract this function,

00:30:23.146 --> 00:30:31.280
at the points where there were peaks (these are powers of two minus one),

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:36.053
we get exactly 0 at these points.

00:30:36.053 --> 00:30:45.658
When n equals 2^(k-1), this expression becomes 2^k.

00:30:45.658 --> 00:30:50.000
And here, I multiplied it by Log[p],

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:59.749
to get the contribution of the prime number p to vp of the product of b_i's.

00:30:59.749 --> 00:31:06.483
The contribution to the logarithm of the product of b_i's.

00:31:06.483 --> 00:31:10.000
I'm just giving you the answer.

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:13.818
We'll try to derive it below.

00:31:13.818 --> 00:31:16.731
Basically, you wouldn't have to guess it,

00:31:16.731 --> 00:31:22.711
you could find it through trial and error.

00:31:22.711 --> 00:31:27.155
If everything is clear with the maxima,

00:31:27.155 --> 00:31:33.132
it's much more complicated with the minima (these points at the bottom).

00:31:33.132 --> 00:31:39.152
Let's take a look at them...

00:31:39.152 --> 00:31:44.328
For this, we will divide the expression we had by n.

00:31:44.328 --> 00:31:51.011
This is a very natural desire when you see such a plot, to divide it by n.

00:31:51.011 --> 00:31:53.924
Here's what we get.

00:31:53.924 --> 00:31:59.100
It appears that this entire plot resides within a certain band.

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:03.471
Both the upper and lower bounds will eventually give us

00:32:03.471 --> 00:32:11.807
upper and lower estimates for Pi[n] or some other prime-related function.

00:32:11.807 --> 00:32:13.413
We'll see this later.

00:32:13.413 --> 00:32:19.544
Right now, we're interested in studying this fractal function and understanding how it behaves.

00:32:19.544 --> 00:32:29.084
Here, I've written down the values of n for all these minima.

00:32:29.084 --> 00:32:33.292
If for points of maximum, we have (2^k)-1

00:32:33.292 --> 00:32:37.253
and the binary representation looks like this,

00:32:37.253 --> 00:32:39.600
then for minima, the answer is like this:

00:32:39.600 --> 00:32:49.058
The binary representation of n looks like 1010101, and so on.

00:32:49.058 --> 00:32:52.999
Well... there's a mistake here...

00:32:52.999 --> 00:32:56.264
In general, it alternates between 1 and 0.

00:32:56.264 --> 00:32:58.918
And sometimes at the end, you encounter an irregularity.

00:32:58.918 --> 00:33:03.289
Sometimes this final "1" is omitted.

00:33:03.289 --> 00:33:07.501
Lets make sure of this

00:33:07.501 --> 00:33:09.555
Here we go...

00:33:09.555 --> 00:33:14.339
So it ends either with (1, 0, 0) or (0, 1, 0).

00:33:14.339 --> 00:33:19.716
Essentially, each subsequent number is either 2 times the previous one

00:33:19.716 --> 00:33:22.695
or 2 times the previous one plus 2.

00:33:22.695 --> 00:33:26.664
Like for example 42 is 20 * 2 + 2.

00:33:26.664 --> 00:33:30.784
This is how the points of minima are arranged.

00:33:30.784 --> 00:33:35.304
This x. And what is y at the points of minima?

00:33:35.304 --> 00:33:44.502
Well, here we get expressions, the structure of which is somewhat clear.

00:33:44.502 --> 00:33:51.362
Firstly, we see that everything tends to some limit -0.14...

00:33:53.976 --> 00:33:55.993
This is a complex matter.

00:33:55.993 --> 00:33:59.586
These numbers are not easy to decipher right away.

00:33:59.586 --> 00:34:02.742
We'll skip studying these numbers.

00:34:02.742 --> 00:34:06.057
Let it be for your independent study.

00:34:09.275 --> 00:34:15.487
So, for powers of two, the upper bound is this,

00:34:15.487 --> 00:34:19.699
and the lower bound is this.

00:34:19.699 --> 00:34:23.631
Here, 0.20744

00:34:23.631 --> 00:34:32.016
is actually this number, but you need to divide it by Log[2].

00:34:32.016 --> 00:34:40.925
Because here, 0.14 is got after multiplying by Log[p].

00:34:40.925 --> 00:34:45.688
And here, I didn't multiply by Log[p].

00:34:45.688 --> 00:34:49.698
For other prime numbers, you get an upper bound like this.

00:34:49.698 --> 00:34:55.863
This is also an analytical result that we will prove below.

00:34:55.863 --> 00:35:03.927
And this result is not proven, but it's somewhat visible.

00:35:03.927 --> 00:35:10.320
You see, this 0.5 – it's an estimate that simply can't be improved upon.

00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:22.256
This value emerges in the very first minimum when p approaches infinity.

00:35:22.256 --> 00:35:25.159
Now, the first minimum looks quite simple.

00:35:25.159 --> 00:35:34.156
It's a scenario where vp[b1, b2, b3, ..., b_n0] doesn't contain the prime number p at all.

00:35:34.156 --> 00:35:43.385
Because there's no prime number p in either the numerator or the denominator as long as n ≤ n0.

00:35:43.385 --> 00:35:49.164
So, let's take n = (p - 1)/2.

00:35:49.164 --> 00:35:52.680
First we need to define...

00:35:52.680 --> 00:35:55.090
Then set...

00:35:55.090 --> 00:35:57.349
Then calculate the limit.

00:35:57.349 --> 00:36:00.262
There we have it. It equals 0.5.

00:36:00.262 --> 00:36:04.381
Of course, this isn't a proof; it's just how the first minimum is structured.

00:36:04.381 --> 00:36:09.677
It's quite possible that the second and third minima will be even smaller.

00:36:09.677 --> 00:36:12.339
This isn't proven here.

00:36:12.339 --> 00:36:19.705
Of course, the number 0.5 for each individual prime number can be replaced by something smaller modulo.

00:36:19.705 --> 00:36:25.111
Now, I'll draw a plot, and hopefully, it'll all become clearer.

00:36:25.111 --> 00:36:38.291
Here, we calculate the minimum for a specific prime number in the range from 0 to 16000.

00:36:38.291 --> 00:36:44.035
Here, you can see the first 15 prime numbers and the number 71.

00:36:44.035 --> 00:36:46.614
For some reason, I did it this way...

00:36:46.614 --> 00:36:50.496
Initially, for two -&gt; 0.20

00:36:50.496 --> 00:36:57.774
Then, it gets closer to 0.5

00:36:57.774 --> 00:37:05.042
For example, this is what the plot looks like for p = 5.

00:37:05.042 --> 00:37:08.966
The first minimum is found right here.

00:37:08.966 --> 00:37:15.319
The second and third minima are at approximately the same level, around -0.4.

00:37:15.319 --> 00:37:20.336
If we take 7... it sort of looks like this.

00:37:20.336 --> 00:37:22.897
Let's create a Manipulate.

00:37:22.897 --> 00:37:26.364
And visualize our difference.

00:37:26.364 --> 00:37:33.080
True minus our approximation (n+1)*Log[n+1].

00:37:33.080 --> 00:37:38.507
This function is simply 0. A horizontal line.

00:37:38.507 --> 00:37:46.085
And this is another horizontal line, which is derived...

00:37:46.085 --> 00:37:48.638
right here.

00:37:48.638 --> 00:37:52.821
Some unique constant for each prime number.

00:37:52.821 --> 00:38:03.074
And this constant is determined simply as the actual minimum among the first 16000 n values.

00:38:03.074 --> 00:38:12.621
Let's experiment... p equals 2. The first prime number.

00:38:12.621 --> 00:38:18.306
Three... Already -0.4...

00:38:18.306 --> 00:38:23.978
Then, smaller modulo...

00:38:23.978 --> 00:38:25.936
Even smaller modulo...

00:38:25.936 --> 00:38:32.369
So, p = 7 is the smallest.

00:38:32.369 --> 00:38:38.780
And then it gradually starts increasing again.

00:38:38.780 --> 00:38:44.413
As p increases, this minimum grows very slowly.

00:38:44.413 --> 00:38:52.368
The first minimum increases very slowly and approaches -0.5 as p tends to infinity.

00:38:52.368 --> 00:38:58.788
And as we can see from examples of small prime numbers,

00:38:58.788 --> 00:39:07.877
the second, third and subsequent minima don't deviate much from the first minimum.

00:39:07.877 --> 00:39:15.451
A similar study of minima can be done for p = 3 and other values of p.

00:39:15.451 --> 00:39:20.251
Maxima are found at points (p^k) - 1.

00:39:20.251 --> 00:39:25.578
In these n values.

00:39:25.578 --> 00:39:35.128
And the pi-notation of these numbers looks like a sequence of twos for p = 3.

00:39:35.128 --> 00:39:39.169
For p = 5, it would be fours.

00:39:39.169 --> 00:39:42.434
Minima are a more complex matter.

00:39:42.434 --> 00:39:46.805
I zoomed in here once again,

00:39:46.805 --> 00:39:49.618
looked at what n equals,

00:39:49.618 --> 00:39:56.201
and wrote down the initial values. 13, 40, 121, etc.

00:39:56.201 --> 00:39:59.446
Let's see how they are structured. They are structured very simply.

00:39:59.446 --> 00:40:04.923
This number, in pi-notation, consists of nothing but ones.

00:40:04.923 --> 00:40:10.000
Here is where the minimum is located.

00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:18.137
You can see that the numerical values are slowly increasing modulo,

00:40:18.137 --> 00:40:21.677
and most likely, they have some limit

00:40:21.677 --> 00:40:27.254
that is less than 0.5 modulo.

00:40:27.254 --> 00:40:30.871
Studying these minima is a complex matter.

00:40:30.871 --> 00:40:36.470
In short, one can delve into the study of minima for different p

00:40:36.470 --> 00:40:42.450
and try to guess what this limit of minima is.

00:40:42.450 --> 00:40:44.051
What I want to draw your attention to

00:40:44.051 --> 00:40:52.444
I want to draw your attention to the fact that the contributions of different prime numbers to Accumulate[vp[b[i]],

00:40:52.444 --> 00:40:55.709
these contributions are roughly the same.

00:40:55.709 --> 00:41:01.991
At first, the contributions from different prime numbers to the logarithm of Accumulate[vp]

00:41:01.991 --> 00:41:06.203
They sort of keep overtaking each other.

00:41:06.203 --> 00:41:07.910
I'm not sure if this is a correct statement...

00:41:07.910 --> 00:41:13.186
The statement that for any two prime numbers, their contributions constantly overtake each other.

00:41:13.186 --> 00:41:19.711
And no contribution ever dominates the other completely, to infinity.

00:41:19.711 --> 00:41:24.765
The number p = 2, it seems, is a special case after all.

00:41:24.765 --> 00:41:26.974
But the others...

00:41:26.974 --> 00:41:30.000
With the others, it's complicated.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:34.321
Now, let me explain.

00:41:34.321 --> 00:41:35.977
Going back a bit.

00:41:35.977 --> 00:41:39.113
Look at what we've got.

00:41:39.113 --> 00:41:48.069
We've got that the contribution from the prime number p is equal to its vp multiplied by the logarithm.

00:41:48.069 --> 00:41:51.183
Let's take this value.

00:41:51.183 --> 00:41:54.850
Multiply it by Log[p].

00:41:54.850 --> 00:42:00.327
And what do we get when we bring this logarithm of p inside the brackets?

00:42:00.327 --> 00:42:01.226
Let's bring it in...

00:42:01.226 --> 00:42:06.513
Here, p disappears and it becomes a simple natural logarithm.

00:42:06.513 --> 00:42:08.679
And here, multiplication takes place.

00:42:08.679 --> 00:42:12.849
So, the contribution of the prime number p,

00:42:12.849 --> 00:42:18.519
it's mostly (n + 1) * Log[n+1] for any prime number p.

00:42:18.519 --> 00:42:25.856
In other words, the contributions from all prime numbers are asymptotically the same, (n + 1) Log[n+1],

00:42:25.856 --> 00:42:30.459
if we ignore this small addition, which does depend on p,

00:42:30.459 --> 00:42:33.523
but it's noticeably smaller than this.

00:42:33.523 --> 00:42:42.109
So, we can kind of disregard it and roughly say that the contributions are the same.

00:42:42.109 --> 00:42:46.064
And maybe even the statement that they constantly overtake each other is true for large p.

00:42:46.064 --> 00:42:51.994
Because this number appears to approach zero.

00:42:51.994 --> 00:42:57.079
I'm talking about the limit of Log[p] / (p - 1) -&gt; 0 as p -&gt; infinity (without the n multiplier).

00:42:57.079 --> 00:43:00.000
Because this number approaches zero

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:05.144
and against the backdrop of this number, 0.5 or 0.4, whatever it is,

00:43:05.144 --> 00:43:09.350
it diminishes to zero for large p.

00:43:09.350 --> 00:43:19.789
And large prime numbers, they definitely behave this way, overtaking each other within the b_i's.

00:43:19.789 --> 00:43:24.124
Alright... Let's move on.

00:43:24.124 --> 00:43:30.896
Let's now move on to our other important statement.

00:43:30.896 --> 00:43:33.821
To this first statement.

00:43:33.821 --> 00:43:46.538
And now, let's write that the contribution of the prime number p to the logarithm of b1, b2, b3, ..., bn

00:43:46.538 --> 00:43:52.661
is less than or equal to this.

00:43:52.661 --> 00:44:00.501
Because b_i has an estimate like this, what can do?

00:44:00.501 --> 00:44:06.734
We can write this inequality, simplifying b_i to an estimate of 2^2n.

00:44:06.734 --> 00:44:12.633
There's an error here. We should use Stirling's formula and estimate it in the other direction.
Instead of an upper estimate
b[n] &lt; 4^n
we need a lower estimate like
b[n] &gt; 3.9^n, starting from some n.
 
Or work with O[n], as done below.

00:44:14.493 --> 00:44:17.564
Let's go through with it.

00:44:17.564 --> 00:44:21.515
From this statement about the approximation of b_n

00:44:21.515 --> 00:44:26.389
and from what I call the vp-identity,

00:44:26.389 --> 00:44:29.101
we get:

00:44:29.101 --> 00:44:36.187
the logarithm of the product is equal to n(n+1)Log[2].

00:44:36.187 --> 00:44:42.945
This follows from the approximation that b_i equals 4^i.

00:44:42.945 --> 00:44:52.598
And from this statement, by replacing Log[p] * vp

00:44:52.598 --> 00:44:56.064
with this expression, multiplied by 1/2,

00:44:56.064 --> 00:45:00.936
we get this answer.

00:45:00.936 --> 00:45:05.006
Since the contribution of each prime number is approximately the same,

00:45:05.006 --> 00:45:08.162
we end up with the count of prime numbers

00:45:08.162 --> 00:45:15.801
that enter into the products of b_i from 1 to n

00:45:15.801 --> 00:45:18.865
multiplied by the size of this contribution.

00:45:18.865 --> 00:45:23.989
We have these two things, and now we write the inequality.

00:45:23.989 --> 00:45:26.349
We need to figure out which way it's directed.

00:45:26.349 --> 00:45:31.238
It turns out that Pi[2n] is greater than or equal to

00:45:31.238 --> 00:45:37.396
this estimate

00:45:37.396 --> 00:45:40.312
divided by this estimate,

00:45:40.312 --> 00:45:42.724
which came from here.

00:45:42.724 --> 00:45:48.639
And we get that Pi[2n] is greater than or equal to this thing.

00:45:48.639 --> 00:45:53.315
Or greater than or equal to 0.69n/Log[n].

00:45:53.315 --> 00:46:00.536
That's how we made a step towards the prime number theorem.

00:46:00.536 --> 00:46:02.673
We didn't prove that this limit exists,

00:46:02.673 --> 00:46:08.703
but we did prove that this quantity is bounded from below by the number 0.69.

00:46:11.010 --> 00:46:15.616
I'll skip this part, it seems like some nonsense is written here...

00:46:16.340 --> 00:46:20.000
What else I want to tell you here...

00:46:20.000 --> 00:46:35.468
Well, okay... Let's move on to the attempt to prove that the maximum approaches (n + 1) Logₚ[n+1].

00:46:35.468 --> 00:46:43.124
For this, we'll need to draw generating functions for triangles.

00:46:43.124 --> 00:46:55.652
We've already drawn a triangle for the frequency values of vp[b[i]] for p = 2.

00:46:55.652 --> 00:46:59.750
It turns out that this is, indeed, Pascal's Triangle.

00:46:59.750 --> 00:47:01.236
So...

00:47:04.956 --> 00:47:17.991
The triangles for p = 3, 5 and 7 look like this.

00:47:17.991 --> 00:47:20.626
A very interesting puzzle is to try to crack this triangle.

00:47:20.626 --> 00:47:25.817
To figure out if there are any recurrent dependencies here.

00:47:25.817 --> 00:47:28.441
The answer is: "There are!"

00:47:28.441 --> 00:47:33.747
Moreover, we can choose different options for the recurrent formula.

00:47:33.747 --> 00:47:40.274
For example, we can represent any number in this triangle as a linear combination of numbers

00:47:40.274 --> 00:47:43.983
that are right here.

00:47:43.983 --> 00:47:46.572
The coefficients in this linear combination turn out to be:

00:47:46.572 --> 00:47:50.804
2, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and so on.

00:47:50.804 --> 00:47:53.076
Let me demonstrate how this works.

00:47:53.076 --> 00:48:03.230
1072 – this is a sum of these numbers with coefficients 2, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16.

00:48:03.230 --> 00:48:06.647
In Mathematica, the dot corresponds to scalar multiplication.

00:48:06.647 --> 00:48:11.984
And you get exactly linear combinations of numbers from this series

00:48:11.984 --> 00:48:16.813
with coefficients from this series.

00:48:16.813 --> 00:48:19.334
You can choose a different option for the recurrent formula.

00:48:19.334 --> 00:48:20.530
You can write that this number

00:48:20.530 --> 00:48:22.932
depends as the sum of these numbers.

00:48:22.932 --> 00:48:27.677
And, oddly enough, the coefficients will be the same powers of two.

00:48:27.677 --> 00:48:33.358
At first, some irregularity (2), and then there are powers of two.

00:48:33.358 --> 00:48:35.930
You can choose this option.

00:48:35.930 --> 00:48:36.889
And write this number

00:48:36.889 --> 00:48:39.000
as a linear combination of these numbers.

00:48:39.000 --> 00:48:47.433
The number 312 should have been included in the yellow strip - into the set of numbers, a linear combination of which gives the number 1072.

00:48:47.885 --> 00:48:51.113
But there, the dependence is more complex.

00:48:51.113 --> 00:48:55.535
You can come up with a recurrent formula like this.

00:48:55.535 --> 00:48:59.629
By the way, the same applies to Pascal's Triangle.

00:48:59.629 --> 00:49:02.930
Just, for example, in this version, it will have...

00:49:02.930 --> 00:49:10.000
It will simply have: the sum of two numbers above with coefficients 1, like [1072] = [312] + [419].

00:49:10.452 --> 00:49:14.723
For instance, here it seems like there will be all ones as coefficients.

00:49:14.723 --> 00:49:20.654
In Pascal's Triangle, you can also choose different options for the recurrent formula.

00:49:20.654 --> 00:49:25.582
Not just the sum of the two above.

00:49:25.582 --> 00:49:28.731
Take a look at this moment.

00:49:28.731 --> 00:49:33.384
In general, here I'm showing that there are different options for the recurrent formula.

00:49:33.384 --> 00:49:37.324
And the simplest one to guess is this formula.

00:49:37.324 --> 00:49:41.663
Here, there are simply powers of two for p = 3.

00:49:41.663 --> 00:49:44.556
And for p = 5...

00:49:44.556 --> 00:49:50.888
Let's see what we get for p = 5...

00:49:50.888 --> 00:49:55.160
We get this 6-th row of the triangle.

00:49:55.160 --> 00:50:00.201
By the way, you can try to derive an explicit formula using some factorials.

00:50:00.201 --> 00:50:05.377
And for this, you can break down these numbers into prime exponents.

00:50:05.377 --> 00:50:08.433
But here, there's nothing interesting to see.

00:50:08.433 --> 00:50:15.634
You can see simple things, like 3^6 – the first number, and 3^5 * 2 – the last number.

00:50:15.634 --> 00:50:20.474
But inside... it's not obvious.

00:50:20.474 --> 00:50:23.381
Here's the triangle for p = 5.

00:50:23.381 --> 00:50:26.101
And here, something interesting happens.

00:50:26.101 --> 00:50:34.436
For example, the number 3970 is written as

00:50:34.436 --> 00:50:41.363
a linear combination of this, this, this, and this number

00:50:41.363 --> 00:50:50.363
with coefficients of 3, and then there's a geometric progression with q = 3.

00:50:50.363 --> 00:50:57.292
Each subsequent number is three times larger than the previous one and starts with 4.

00:50:57.292 --> 00:50:59.200
For p = 7...

00:50:59.200 --> 00:51:04.731
In general, these recurrent formulas are guessed, and then proved.

00:51:04.731 --> 00:51:08.147
And we get the following:

00:51:08.147 --> 00:51:15.706
from these recurrent formulas, we can derive generating functions for the triangles.

00:51:15.706 --> 00:51:21.032
From this generating function, we can get an estimate of the mean value.

00:51:21.032 --> 00:51:22.783
It's very simple.

00:51:22.783 --> 00:51:25.336
So, we have a triangle.

00:51:25.336 --> 00:51:28.702
Let's take a specific fifth row.

00:51:28.702 --> 00:51:37.849
What is the average value of the exponent within this row?

00:51:37.849 --> 00:51:43.158
We take the coefficients from this row as weights.

00:51:43.158 --> 00:51:50.000
So, the total number of elements in the row is the sum of the coefficients.

00:51:50.000 --> 00:51:56.660
This is also the number of indices i that we traverse.

00:51:56.660 --> 00:52:03.210
And in the numerator, we need to write that we get a power of 2 ten times.

00:52:03.210 --> 00:52:06.631
We get a power of 3 ten times.

00:52:06.631 --> 00:52:08.796
We get a power of 4 five times.

00:52:08.796 --> 00:52:12.549
And we get a power of 5 once.

00:52:12.549 --> 00:52:23.530
Here's the manually calculated average exponent of two within b_i.

00:52:23.530 --> 00:52:26.831
How could we express this (this expression)

00:52:26.831 --> 00:52:30.224
using the generating function of the row?

00:52:30.224 --> 00:52:31.855
Like this.

00:52:31.855 --> 00:52:38.119
We can take the derivative of this polynomial, and it will do what we need.

00:52:38.119 --> 00:52:40.000
This 10 will be multiplied by 2.

00:52:40.000 --> 00:52:42.000
This 10 will be multiplied by 3.

00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:44.153
This 5 will be multiplied by 4.

00:52:44.153 --> 00:52:47.820
And this 1 will be multiplied by 5.

00:52:51.138 --> 00:52:56.715
Just remember to substitute x with 1.

00:52:56.715 --> 00:52:58.290
It matches.

00:52:58.290 --> 00:53:01.711
This with this.

00:53:01.711 --> 00:53:07.188
This expression, when we take the derivative of the function and divide by the function itself,

00:53:07.188 --> 00:53:11.194
it's the same as taking the derivative of the logarithm of that function.

00:53:11.194 --> 00:53:13.627
It's all simple.

00:53:13.627 --> 00:53:20.564
Our k-th row (the coefficient at y^k) is this thing.

00:53:20.564 --> 00:53:23.139
((1 + x)^k) - 1

00:53:23.139 --> 00:53:28.520
And we can simply calculate what this expression equals.

00:53:28.520 --> 00:53:31.861
The logarithmic derivative at the point x = 1.

00:53:31.861 --> 00:53:34.573
So, we've got the answer for 2.

00:53:34.573 --> 00:53:41.255
And it's clear that gradually it all approaches 0.5.

00:53:41.255 --> 00:53:45.079
Good. Now for 3.

00:53:45.079 --> 00:53:48.329
Here's our triangle.

00:53:48.329 --> 00:53:53.418
Here's the recurrent formula we chose.

00:53:53.418 --> 00:54:05.270
How to get the generating function of the triangle from the recurrent formula?

00:54:05.270 --> 00:54:07.805
Let's introduce the notation f_i.

00:54:07.805 --> 00:54:11.673
Let f_k be the coefficient at y^k.

00:54:11.673 --> 00:54:15.634
It is claimed that from the recurrent formula...

00:54:15.634 --> 00:54:22.197
This one. It's written for a specific case, but it's more convenient in a sense.

00:54:22.197 --> 00:54:35.907
It turns out that f_n[x] can be gotten from 2 * f_n-1[x].

00:54:35.907 --> 00:54:39.722
That's this two.

00:54:39.722 --> 00:54:41.079
Plus...

00:54:41.079 --> 00:54:47.059
We take the same power of x, the same vertical, but the previous row,

00:54:47.059 --> 00:54:50.969
and the previous row is n - 1.

00:54:50.969 --> 00:54:54.486
Then we take the pre-previous row, n - 2.

00:54:54.486 --> 00:55:00.000
But here, we need to shift to the left, so here we have a multiplication by x.

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:02.722
To make this number appear as a power of x,

00:55:02.722 --> 00:55:11.761
which is this, you need to multiply f_n-2 by x.

00:55:11.761 --> 00:55:16.517
Next, here comes the 2. It's from the pre-previous row already.

00:55:16.517 --> 00:55:20.486
Then 4, then 8 and so on.

00:55:20.486 --> 00:55:33.479
So, it turns out that F[x, y] (now we're thinking about F[x, y]) can be gotten from F[x, y] in the following way:

00:55:33.479 --> 00:55:38.447
2y means we take the previous row.

00:55:38.447 --> 00:55:41.008
In other words, we multiply by "y".

00:55:41.008 --> 00:55:52.119
This means that now something from y^k-1 from the same triangle will contribute to y^k.

00:55:52.119 --> 00:56:03.068
Then, we take from the n - 2 row by multiplying the same function F[x, y] by y^2.

00:56:03.068 --> 00:56:09.364
And we also need to shift to the left, so the multiplication by x occurs.

00:56:09.364 --> 00:56:11.878
What's here?

00:56:11.878 --> 00:56:13.735
That's a good question.

00:56:13.735 --> 00:56:17.905
It's just found by trial and error to figure out what to substitute here.

00:56:17.905 --> 00:56:24.178
In the end, we get this expression.

00:56:24.178 --> 00:56:32.724
Here are calculations where I was specifically finding this expression that needs to be substituted in the numerators.

00:56:32.724 --> 00:56:39.305
And the denominator follows from the recurrent formula.

00:56:39.305 --> 00:56:52.744
Thus, for p = 3, we get the generating function of the frequency triangle as follows.

00:56:52.744 --> 00:56:55.722
Here, I'm just making sure the answer is correct.

00:56:55.722 --> 00:57:00.926
And these coefficients are exactly what I saw in the triangle,

00:57:00.926 --> 00:57:06.596
computed explicitly by hand in the real triangle.

00:57:06.596 --> 00:57:12.007
Okay... This is all very complicated and will take forever if I don't skip ahead.

00:57:12.007 --> 00:57:21.129
Similar things can be done for p = 5, 7, 11 and we get the answer.

00:57:21.129 --> 00:57:23.998
The answer is written here.

00:57:23.998 --> 00:57:36.695
We find that for the binomial coefficients triangle of prime frequencies for n = p^k,

00:57:36.695 --> 00:57:41.812
it can be expressed by this generating function.

00:57:41.812 --> 00:57:51.205
So how do we proceed from here? How do we get the mean value of the exponent from this answer?

00:57:51.205 --> 00:57:58.462
We take the denominator and study its roots with respect to y.

00:57:58.462 --> 00:58:07.200
Knowing the roots, we can expand it into a combination of two such simple fractions.

00:58:07.200 --> 00:58:11.497
1 divided by y minus the first root.

00:58:11.497 --> 00:58:13.455
This scary root here.

00:58:13.455 --> 00:58:19.601
1 divided by y minus y2. The second scary root.

00:58:19.601 --> 00:58:22.766
And when you expand such an expression,

00:58:22.766 --> 00:58:32.567
you get some linear combination of simple fractions like 1/(y - y_i).

00:58:32.567 --> 00:58:35.203
The coefficients depend on the situation here.

00:58:35.203 --> 00:58:38.418
Here, we have coefficients of 1 and -1.

00:58:38.418 --> 00:58:44.154
And then we divide by this thing, independent of y.

00:58:44.154 --> 00:58:47.485
Lets check...

00:58:47.485 --> 00:58:50.201
It's true.

00:58:50.201 --> 00:58:54.281
This is just 1 divided by the denominator.

00:58:54.281 --> 00:58:58.743
And if we have some numerator, we simply add it.

00:58:58.743 --> 00:59:02.582
Based on this expansion, we will now get the answer.

00:59:02.582 --> 00:59:07.080
It’s clear what these things are equal to.

00:59:07.080 --> 00:59:12.557
These are basically formulas of infinite geometric progressions.

00:59:12.557 --> 00:59:14.477
So here, we write:

00:59:14.477 --> 00:59:20.659
one geometric progression minus the second geometric progression.

00:59:20.659 --> 00:59:23.172
We take this coefficient.

00:59:23.172 --> 00:59:25.917
Checking...

00:59:25.917 --> 00:59:32.191
Okay. I wrote this for 5. Let's go back to 5...

00:59:32.191 --> 00:59:36.210
Checking...

00:59:36.210 --> 00:59:39.799
Fourth row for p = 5.

00:59:39.799 --> 00:59:42.726
81, 189

00:59:42.726 --> 00:59:48.609
Here I got the answer for this thing.

00:59:51.475 --> 00:59:59.782
To get the answer for this thing, you need to do a simple thing.

01:00:00.586 --> 01:00:09.307
The coefficients b_n, which are here at y^n,

01:00:09.307 --> 01:00:13.134
are gotten by this formula

01:00:13.134 --> 01:00:18.100
from the coefficients of this thing.

01:00:18.100 --> 01:00:20.233
Let's check...

01:00:20.233 --> 01:00:23.138
And indeed, this thing equals

01:00:23.138 --> 01:00:24.987
this expression

01:00:24.987 --> 01:00:29.149
minus x times this expression.

01:00:29.149 --> 01:00:35.847
162 = 189 - 27

01:00:35.847 --> 01:00:43.042
First, we manually calculate the number we believe in.

01:00:43.042 --> 01:00:46.702
Then, we verify.

01:00:46.702 --> 01:00:51.095
Yes... the correct answer.

01:00:52.352 --> 01:00:55.356
And here comes the moment of truth.

01:00:55.356 --> 01:01:05.207
What is the average value of the degree in the k-th row of the triangle for prime number p &gt; 2?

01:01:05.207 --> 01:01:08.766
It all simplifies to this form.

01:01:08.766 --> 01:01:11.352
And here are the answers.

01:01:11.352 --> 01:01:16.989
The average value for n = p^k.

01:01:16.989 --> 01:01:23.262
The average value for n = (p^k) - 1, which is more accurate, as it turns out, it's different.

01:01:23.262 --> 01:01:29.736
We need to multiply this by p^k and divide by (p^k) - 1.

01:01:29.736 --> 01:01:37.232
Because, as we remember, vp of b^n, where n = p^k, equals 0.

01:01:37.232 --> 01:01:41.368
So, we can either include b_n or not.

01:01:41.368 --> 01:01:42.915
It doesn't matter.

01:01:42.915 --> 01:01:49.720
For this n, b_n has a zero power of the prime number p inside.

01:01:49.720 --> 01:01:54.870
Oh... this is probably quite hard to follow just by listening.

01:01:54.870 --> 01:02:00.932
Next... This is for 2. For n = (2^k) - 1.

01:02:00.932 --> 01:02:08.428
And here is not the average but the sum of the powers of prime number p inside b_i's.

01:02:08.428 --> 01:02:10.724
Similar expressions are gotten.

01:02:10.724 --> 01:02:17.752
Only for p &gt; 2, this adjustment comes into play.

01:02:17.752 --> 01:02:20.738
Here below, I explain what I said verbally.

01:02:20.738 --> 01:02:27.570
That vp[b_p^k] = 0. We discussed this earlier.

01:02:27.570 --> 01:02:29.729
And that vp of this product

01:02:29.729 --> 01:02:32.885
matches vp of this product as the results.

01:02:32.885 --> 01:02:38.150
And these are the functions I wrote down at the bottom.

01:02:38.150 --> 01:02:42.596
And here's what we get for these specific n.

01:02:42.596 --> 01:02:50.000
Such answer. Absolute equality when n = (p^k) - 1.

01:02:50.000 --> 01:02:53.053
Why is it a maximum?

01:02:58.281 --> 01:03:03.103
Alright... Let's make this a homework question: Why do we need to use the ≤ sign here?

01:03:03.103 --> 01:03:08.329
Why are these n the points of maximum

01:03:08.329 --> 01:03:13.244
of the powers of prime number p inside the product?

01:03:13.244 --> 01:03:17.394
We started with quite elementary methods.

01:03:17.394 --> 01:03:23.519
We simply became interested in the powers of prime numbers inside Catalan numbers.

01:03:23.519 --> 01:03:29.386
And then we went in the direction where the road opened up to us.

01:03:29.386 --> 01:03:37.478
Of course, you need some experience to just ask yourself such a question,

01:03:37.478 --> 01:03:40.341
to make such an observation about prime numbers.

01:03:40.341 --> 01:03:47.480
"Let's look at the product of all prime numbers in the range from n to 2n".

01:03:47.480 --> 01:03:55.147
Or "let's examine the contributions of different prime numbers inside the product of b_i's".

01:03:55.147 --> 01:04:04.797
You need to know how to ask such questions, and that's the main secret of mathematical creativity.

01:04:04.797 --> 01:04:09.388
Then, it's a matter of technique and perseverance.

01:04:09.388 --> 01:04:15.174
As it turned out, Catalan numbers and central binomial coefficients

01:04:15.174 --> 01:04:26.506
can shed light on the asymptotics of these functions, to both lower and upper bounds.

01:04:26.506 --> 01:04:32.447
We used the simple fact that any number x is the product of prime numbers.

01:04:32.447 --> 01:04:40.547
But after taking logarithms, it's expressed in this more complex form.

01:04:40.547 --> 01:04:53.061
And based on the triangle of frequency of powers of the prime number p inside b_i's, we derived some estimates.

01:04:53.061 --> 01:05:01.102
And an important intermediate step was getting the generating function for the frequency triangles.

01:05:01.102 --> 01:05:11.276
And now we know how to bound this A[n], which is part of the formula for Pi[n].

01:05:11.276 --> 01:05:14.901
Pi[n] is approximately n/Log[n].

01:05:14.901 --> 01:05:21.082
And we have proven that the multiplier A[n], which is determined simply by this equation,

01:05:21.082 --> 01:05:25.093
at some point, starting from some n, begins to fluctuate within this range.

01:05:25.093 --> 01:05:34.300
The truth lies in the fact that the limit of A[n] is 1. And this is the fundamental prime number theorem.

01:05:34.300 --> 01:05:44.051
It was proven in 1896 using the Riemann zeta function.

01:05:44.051 --> 01:05:55.917
And essentially, this theorem is equivalent to proving that the Riemann zeta function has a pole at 1.

01:05:55.917 --> 01:05:58.168
A pole of degree 1.

01:05:58.168 --> 01:06:07.849
And that all zeros of this function are to the left of the vertical line with real part equal to 1.

01:06:07.849 --> 01:06:10.624
Chebyshev proved a very interesting statement.

01:06:10.624 --> 01:06:12.632
He was close to this limit.

01:06:12.632 --> 01:06:17.506
He proved that if the limit exists, it is equal to 1.

01:06:17.506 --> 01:06:21.944
But the existence of this limit turned out to be a big question.

01:06:21.944 --> 01:06:28.881
And it turns out that now we have proofs based on the theory of complex variables.

01:06:28.881 --> 01:06:38.948
It's quite unusual that theorems about prime numbers require knowledge of all this.

01:06:38.948 --> 01:06:43.409
Analytic continuations and the theory of complex variables.

01:06:43.409 --> 01:06:45.554
Now it's no longer a surprise.

01:06:45.554 --> 01:06:54.048
Perhaps, back then, it was such an interesting connection between two areas of mathematics.

01:06:54.048 --> 01:07:03.899
We have many unanswered questions about the exact value of this lower bound δ_p.

01:07:03.899 --> 01:07:12.477
And about the average value of this fractal function.

01:07:12.477 --> 01:07:19.010
In some sense, a simpler approach to proving these inequalities.

01:07:19.010 --> 01:07:22.024
You can find it online here, for example.

01:07:22.024 --> 01:07:27.032
An excellent course on analytic number theory.

01:07:27.032 --> 01:07:30.000
It's such... systematic. It starts with the theorem.

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:37.242
You have to prove the theorem, and somehow, you start pulling lemmas out of thin air

01:07:37.242 --> 01:07:40.463
to reach the theorem you need.

01:07:40.463 --> 01:07:45.554
But it happens much faster. Much clearer.

01:07:45.554 --> 01:07:51.949
Everything there is based on Abel's summation formula.

01:07:51.949 --> 01:07:57.249
Stirling's formula is also a special case of Abel's summation formula.

01:07:57.249 --> 01:08:02.224
I recommend you study this course.

01:08:02.224 --> 01:08:05.131
I watched it myself. I liked it a lot!

01:08:05.131 --> 01:08:11.949
However, it's funny that on the second lecture, the sound disappears halfway through.

01:08:11.949 --> 01:08:15.897
You watch the most important part with prove with no sound.

01:08:15.897 --> 01:08:20.796
That's it for today! Good luck to everyone! Goodbye!

